```sql
CREATE DATABASE TripsDB;
USE TripsDB;
```

`CREATE DATABASE` creates the empty database and `USE TripsDB;` selects it so that the following statements apply to it (MySQL / MariaDB syntax; in Microsoft SQL Server `USE TripsDB;` is also used; in PostgreSQL the database is created with `CREATE DATABASE TripsDB;` and selected by connecting to it, for example `\c tripsdb`; SQLite has no `CREATE DATABASE`, the database is the file opened).

```sql
INSERT INTO TEACHER (TeacherID, Title, FirstName, Surname) VALUES ('T1', 'Mr.',  'John',   'Book');
INSERT INTO TEACHER (TeacherID, Title, FirstName, Surname) VALUES ('T2', 'Mrs.', 'Mbong',  'Ngum');
INSERT INTO TEACHER (TeacherID, Title, FirstName, Surname) VALUES ('T3', 'Mr.',  'Aminde', 'Tom');
```

TEACHER is filled first because TRIP refers to it. Check: `SELECT * FROM TEACHER;` returns the 3 rows above.

Each trip must carry the identifier of a teacher that exists in TEACHER (T1, T2 or T3); otherwise the foreign key is violated. Four sample trips (any 3 to 5 consistent rows are accepted):

```sql
INSERT INTO TRIP (TripID, StartDate, EndDate, Destination, TeacherID) VALUES ('TR01', '2023-02-10', '2023-02-12', 'Limbe',   'T1');
INSERT INTO TRIP (TripID, StartDate, EndDate, Destination, TeacherID) VALUES ('TR02', '2023-03-05', '2023-03-06', 'Kribi',   'T2');
INSERT INTO TRIP (TripID, StartDate, EndDate, Destination, TeacherID) VALUES ('TR03', '2023-04-14', '2023-04-16', 'Buea',    'T1');
INSERT INTO TRIP (TripID, StartDate, EndDate, Destination, TeacherID) VALUES ('TR04', '2023-05-20', '2023-05-21', 'Bamenda', 'T3');
```

Teacher T1 supervises two trips (TR01 and TR03), which also gives the queries of (iv) something to find. Dates are in the form 'YYYY-MM-DD' and each EndDate is not before its StartDate. A test insert with a non-existent teacher (for example 'T9') is refused by the DBMS ("FOREIGN KEY constraint failed" in SQLite), which shows that the data is kept consistent.

```sql
INSERT INTO PUPIL (PupilID, PupilFirstName, PupilSurname) VALUES ('P001', 'Neba',   'Ngu');
INSERT INTO PUPIL (PupilID, PupilFirstName, PupilSurname) VALUES ('P002', 'Samira', 'Bih');
INSERT INTO PUPIL (PupilID, PupilFirstName, PupilSurname) VALUES ('P003', 'Amina',  'Abu');
```

The table in the question lists the first name before the surname, whereas the relation is defined as PUPIL(PupilID, PupilSurname, PupilFirstName). Naming the columns in the INSERT statement avoids swapping the two values.

Each row of PUPILTRIP uses a PupilID of PUPIL (P001 to P003) and a TripID of TRIP (TR01 to TR04), and no pair appears twice (composite primary key). PUPIL and TRIP must be filled before this table.

```sql
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P001', 'TR01');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P002', 'TR01');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P003', 'TR01');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P001', 'TR02');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P001', 'TR03');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P002', 'TR03');
INSERT INTO PUPILTRIP (PupilID, TripID) VALUES ('P003', 'TR04');
```

This gives 7 rows: pupil P001 goes on three trips (TR01, TR02, TR03), P002 on two (TR01, TR03), P003 on two (TR01, TR04), and trip TR01 is attended by three pupils: both "many" sides of the many-to-many relationship are illustrated.
